Non-slide notes.
* It's probably possible to speed up non-fixed case with log10 and then a per-log10 routine.
* log10 can be found with "count leading zero" instruction and a small lookup table
  * or even using a parallel divide (mult by recip) and then CLZ to find not only the answer
    but the number of 0s at front

----

Modernisation research (May 2026)

Facet dynamic_cast fix:
* The slide previously said "Fixed in GCC 11!" - this is wrong.
* The fix is libstdc++ commit b3ac43a3, "Avoid redundant checks in
  std::use_facet [PR103755]", by Jonathan Wakely, 9 Nov 2022.
* It introduces __try_use_facet (pointer-returning helper used by both
  has_facet and use_facet) and the _GLIBCXX_STD_FACET macro listing the
  standard facets that get the static_cast fast path.
* Verified on the release branches:
  - releases/gcc-11: still uses dynamic_cast
  - releases/gcc-12: still uses dynamic_cast
  - releases/gcc-13: has _GLIBCXX_STD_FACET / static_cast
* So the fix first ships in GCC 13.1 (April 2023). Slide updated.
* Follow-up commit 952e67c0 (May 2024) extends the fast path to
  const-qualified facet types.

std::format:
* C++20 alternative to stringstream/sprintf. Type-safe, no format-string
  footguns. Compiles cleanly with g161 -std=c++23 -Wall -Wextra -Werror.
* Costs: returning std::string still allocates; the asm output is
  ~25k lines of templated machinery vs ~9 lines for sprintf - hot path
  is fine but it's not "free".
* std::format_to(buf, ...) gives the in-place variant if you already
  own a buffer (closer to take-two's sprintf version).
* Kept as a bonus slide (someone always asks).

----

Re-benchmarked May 2026, Intel i7-10510U @ 1.8GHz, -O2 -std=c++23:

                  GCC 12.5    GCC 13.4    GCC 15.2
  first  (sstream)  ~280 ns     ~310 ns     ~310 ns
  second (sprintf)    92 ns      118 ns      117 ns
  third  (Format)     19 ns       19 ns       18 ns
  fourth              21 ns       24 ns       24 ns
  fifth  (best)       14 ns       14 ns        7 ns

Notes:
* The GCC 11->13 facet dynamic_cast fix doesn't visibly help the
  stringstream benchmark on this microarch - allocation/string
  copy costs dominate. The fix is real and observable in profiles
  (no more __dynamic_cast), but the wall-clock win is in the noise here.
* sprintf got ~25% slower from GCC 12 to 13 - haven't traced why,
  may be a glibc/libstdc++ tuning change.
* Slide numbers updated (the-problem 550->310, take-two 130->115,
  take-three 20->18, going-further 13->9) and the 4x sprintf win is
  now closer to ~2.7x with the modern stringstream baseline.

----

std::format / std::format_to benchmark (May 2026, GCC 15.2 -O2):

  std::format       ~118 ns / order
  std::format_to    ~117 ns / order  (-O3: ~115 ns - no real change)
  sprintf           ~115 ns / order  (for comparison)
  custom Format      ~18 ns / order
  best                ~7 ns / order

Conclusion (counter to my prior hypothesis): std::format is roughly
sprintf-speed, NOT custom-Format-speed. The integer-formatting kernel
in libstdc++ does use the standard tricks, but the sink/iterator
type-erasure wrapped around it costs as much as the win, so the
overall throughput lands next to sprintf.

So in the bonus slide: type-safe drop-in replacement for sprintf with
similar performance - good enough for almost everything, but if you're
in the 18 ns / 7 ns regime you still need a hand-rolled formatter.

Files: sixth.cpp (std::format), seventh.cpp (std::format_to), wired
into the Makefile alongside first..fifth.

----

fmtlib followup + GCC 16.1 results (May 2026, taskset -c 3, median of 5):

  GCC 16.1.0  -O2:
    first    (sstream)         ~292 ns
    second   (sprintf)          ~112 ns
    third    (custom Format)     ~16 ns
    fourth                       ~24 ns
    fifth    (best)               ~7 ns
    sixth    (std::format)      ~153 ns
    seventh  (std::format_to)   ~128 ns
    eighth   (fmt::format_to)    ~80 ns

User was right to push on fmtlib: it's clearly faster than std::format
(80 vs 130 ns) - same API, different implementation, less iterator
plumbing. Still ~5x slower than the custom Format though, so the
"library formatters can't beat hand-rolled at this scale" story holds.

Cross-compiler picture (median of 3, less precise):
                  GCC12.5  GCC13.4  GCC15.2  GCC16.1
  first             ~280     ~310     ~310     ~290
  second             ~92     ~118     ~117     ~112
  third              ~19      ~19      ~18      ~16
  fifth              ~14      ~14       ~7       ~7
  sixth             N/A      ~143     ~153     ~153
  seventh           N/A      ~141     ~145     ~128
  eighth            N/A      N/A      N/A       ~80   (libfmt
                                                       host-glibc
                                                       mismatch
                                                       blocks build
                                                       on g13/15)

Notes:
* sixth (std::format) requires GCC 13+ (no <format> in libstdc++ 12).
* eighth fails to build on GCC 13/15 due to a host glibc threading-
  header mismatch with the CE-distributed compiler trees (system
  __GTHREAD_COND_INIT vs libstdc++'s expectations). Fine on GCC 16.
* fifth's 2x speedup at GCC 15+ is real (consistent across runs);
  haven't traced what optimisation kicked in.

Slide numbers refreshed against GCC 16.1.0 (Take One 290, Two 110,
Three 16, Going Further 7).
